  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Assessment mission E-certification Vietnam</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The significant increase in global trade and travel has led to the movement of products carrying potential health risks for animals, plants, and humans. Sanitary and Phytosanitary (SPS) measures, governed by the World Trade Organization (WTO), address these concerns by ensuring the safety of traded agricultural products. The SPS Agreement mandates that countries implement measures based on international standards to protect their economy and environment without creating unnecessary trade barriers. A crucial aspect is the phytosanitary certificate, confirming the inspected products are free from harmful pests. Electronic SPS certificates, standardized by the UN/CEFACT, facilitate trade. Early adopters of this system include New Zealand, Australia, the USA, and the Netherlands. Despite challenges, cooperation initiatives - such as A Dutch mission in 2016- highlighted the need for professional IT administration to overcome challenges. In recent years, New Zealand and Vietnam entered a partnership aiming to enhance e-certification processes. In September 2023, during a Dutch mission to Vietnam, the Vietnamese government indicated that they still would like the support of the Netherlands in this matter, providing an opportunity for cooperation and capacity building in electronic certification through Dutch expertise with e-CertNL.</narrative>
  </description>
